WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.536
Congressman
Kinzinger, it's good to have you with us.

00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:06.673
What do you make of this Mishegas?

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:08.141
Look at the mess.

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:10.910
I think my biggest worry

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:14.080
just for the country
is not really the ballot after ballot.

00:00:14.214 --> 00:00:15.448
And that's just democracy.

00:00:15.448 --> 00:00:16.383
That's a process.

00:00:16.383 --> 00:00:18.885
It's everybody's learning
kind of house rules now.

00:00:19.319 --> 00:00:21.721
It is. What is Kevin willing to give up?

00:00:21.888 --> 00:00:24.457
Because I think what we're seeing in
some of these,

00:00:24.824 --> 00:00:26.526
we don't even know
what a lot of these rule changes are.

00:00:26.526 --> 00:00:29.596
But for instance, one person
to vacate the chair

00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:32.632
it's going to create chaos,
as we've been talking about.

00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:34.934
Here's
the thing that I think we have to take.

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:38.038
When you talk about the five or six
that are kind of never Kevins,

00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:41.174
what's their motivation
so of the 21 that are voting against

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:44.310
Kevin, a significant number of those
like they really believe.

00:00:44.577 --> 00:00:45.512
I would disagree with that,

00:00:45.512 --> 00:00:48.882
but they believe this is a way
to get to whatever their end goal is.

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:52.285
There are a number of people,
particularly these five

00:00:52.285 --> 00:00:55.622
or six, that enjoy being in this position.

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:57.023
They relish

00:00:57.023 --> 00:01:00.693
being the ones standing up, being the ones
kind of throwing a wrench in this.

00:01:00.994 --> 00:01:05.732
And that's what I think the concern is, is
if they end up being only five, that's

00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:09.502
probably for them even a better situation
because they get more attention.

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:12.105
Look at like Matt Gates
know, nominating Donald Trump.

00:01:12.839 --> 00:01:15.375
And I think I know some of those folks
and I can tell you

00:01:15.375 --> 00:01:18.144
they enjoy
being the five of the six people

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:19.579
that are going to deny
Kevin a speakership.

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:21.781
But it doesn't even have to be
because it's Kevin.

00:01:21.781 --> 00:01:23.850
It's because they can be the ones
to do it.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:27.954
And the idea of that, you might get
some of them to at least vote present

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:32.092
So this can finally resolve itself

00:01:34.127 --> 00:01:37.630
I think it's possible
if you get to like ballot 50 or 70.

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:42.068
But I think by then I think enough
and this is kind of what we have to now

00:01:42.068 --> 00:01:46.139
start watching, which you guys have been
discussing is how many folks and the say

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:49.742
in the middle generically and your average
kind of Republican member of Congress

00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:52.612
is going to be worried about
what's been given up.

00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:56.449
They're going to realize that
at this point you're really picking

00:01:56.449 --> 00:02:00.487
a person over the health of the party and
by extension, the health of the country.

00:02:00.753 --> 00:02:03.623
And you start floating
alternative names once alternative names

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:07.727
start getting floated by, we'll call them
the regular members of Congress,

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:08.895
for lack of a better term.

00:02:08.895 --> 00:02:10.263
I think Kevin's done at that point.

00:02:10.263 --> 00:02:11.898
Then he just has it's the problem

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:15.668
with some of these folks that have pledged
only Kevin, I understand the purpose.

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:17.804
It's like, hey,
we're as committed as you guys are,

00:02:18.204 --> 00:02:21.975
but you have to have an out
because this could grind on forever.

00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:24.043
And I don't think
Kevin can win in the long run.

00:02:24.043 --> 00:02:27.413
So so I was going to say, you know,
Congressman, well, that assumes that.

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:31.217
So this is the 20 right left to say
they've been offered something.

00:02:31.217 --> 00:02:34.220
The toothpaste is out of the tube in this
right So how do you put it back in?

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:34.888
Yeah, right.

00:02:34.888 --> 00:02:37.690
So how do you put it back in now and say,
you know,

00:02:38.358 --> 00:02:40.426
Congressman, your point is like
we're going to get somebody else.

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:43.062
But but the 20
if you're Chip Roy, these folks

00:02:43.363 --> 00:02:45.765
you're going to say, well, wait,
we want our chips.

00:02:45.765 --> 00:02:47.901
And we just we just
we just negotiated this.

00:02:48.134 --> 00:02:50.937
The new person is going to come in and
say, that was the old guy, not with me.

00:02:51.404 --> 00:02:55.642
So you know, because I know look, I think
you're I think you're completely right.

00:02:55.675 --> 00:02:58.611
I don't know how let's say let's just
use Scalise as an example.

00:02:58.611 --> 00:03:01.247
Let's say he comes forward and says,
now I want the motion to vacate the chair

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:03.449
not to be one person
they're not going to go for that.

00:03:03.449 --> 00:03:05.685
And that's why
I think it's still not likely.

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:08.421
So I don't want to get people
kind of moving around on this.

00:03:08.421 --> 00:03:11.291
But there is still a possibility,
especially as this drags on,

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:13.293
that you end up coming to this idea

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:16.496
of a consensus candidate,
an acceptable Republican, the Democrats.

00:03:16.696 --> 00:03:19.799
The big question, can you get somebody
like AOC to vote for a Republican?

00:03:20.333 --> 00:03:22.535
That's not for me to answer.
I think it'd be tough for him.

00:03:22.702 --> 00:03:26.639
And then enough Republicans that say, like
we've seen in some of these statehouses,

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.942
let's have
a kind of a coalition government.

00:03:28.942 --> 00:03:30.610
I actually think in the long run,

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:33.513
that would be good
for healing the institution of the House.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:37.116
But how I mean, David Axelrod,
how likely is something like that?

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:40.119
Because the Republicans who would vote

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:43.456
with Democrats would could pay
a big political price for it.

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:47.126
Yeah, Adam, I mean, you'd you
would be someone to answer this question.

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:50.496
I mean, my feeling
is that in this political environment,

00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:54.901
that is a tough decision for Republicans

00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:57.937
to make in the Republican
who takes the the job to make.

00:03:57.937 --> 00:04:00.106
I mean, I I'm with you.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.408
I think that would be good
for the country.

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:04.277
It would be good for the institution.

00:04:04.277 --> 00:04:08.114
It would be a way of breaking
the fever that you've fought to break.

00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:09.649
But the

00:04:09.649 --> 00:04:12.118
question
is, does the politics allow for it

00:04:12.752 --> 00:04:15.388
or do you open up the door to, you know,

00:04:15.388 --> 00:04:18.424
civil war in the primaries again?

00:04:18.424 --> 00:04:20.360
Well, look,
I think if you look historically,

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:22.095
the politics doesn't allow for it.

00:04:22.095 --> 00:04:26.566
But we're in a completely unprecedented
moment, at least in our memories,

00:04:26.566 --> 00:04:31.104
where now I mean, as you have five people
keeping Kevin McCarthy ultimately

00:04:31.104 --> 00:04:34.474
from the speakership, you could have five
that create a coalition government.

00:04:34.474 --> 00:04:37.410
Put yourself in the mindset
of some of these members of Congress.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.345
Let's say they come from a Biden district.

00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:42.482
Let's say they've made a mental decision
that this is their last term

00:04:42.749 --> 00:04:44.984
and now they have an opportunity
to kind of stand up.

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:46.719
Look, it's
not going to be a Democratic speaker.

00:04:46.719 --> 00:04:48.187
And I think that has to be clear.

00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:51.391
But an acceptable Republican
that creates a level playing field

00:04:52.025 --> 00:04:55.261
and that kind of a speaker
could actually create an institution

00:04:55.261 --> 00:04:56.229
that actually works.

00:04:56.229 --> 00:04:59.999
So if you put yourself in the mind,
you only need five or six people

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:04.370
to say it's a historic opportunity
to do something to heal this institution.

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:05.938
I may not be running again.

00:05:05.938 --> 00:05:10.510
And even if I am, I think maybe
in two years the history will judge it.

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:14.080
Well, Congressman Kinzinger,
how how many of those people are there?

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:15.315
I mean, they sound like unicorns.

00:05:15.315 --> 00:05:18.151
How many are they actually do they exist?

00:05:19.519 --> 00:05:20.420
Yeah, they exist.

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:24.257
I mean, you've heard Don Bacon,
for instance, kind of float this idea.

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:24.891
Right.

00:05:24.891 --> 00:05:26.225
Don Bacon is a great guy.

00:05:26.225 --> 00:05:29.395
Don Bacon,
you know, represents his district well.

00:05:30.063 --> 00:05:31.564
He obviously is a military guy.

00:05:31.564 --> 00:05:34.434
There are people with his kind of,

00:05:34.434 --> 00:05:38.137
you know, resume sort of there
in the in the House of Representatives

00:05:38.137 --> 00:05:39.472
that I don't think we're there yet.

00:05:39.472 --> 00:05:41.007
I don't think it's
going to happen tomorrow.

00:05:41.007 --> 00:05:44.610
But as this thing drags on, particularly
as they get concerned with

00:05:44.877 --> 00:05:48.815
these institutional things
and again, Americans have to be thinking

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:51.718
what are we giving up for our country

00:05:51.984 --> 00:05:54.754
so that one single man
can become speaker of the House?

00:05:55.321 --> 00:05:56.289
And and I think

00:05:56.289 --> 00:05:59.325
as that gets into those people's minds,
that might trigger something.

00:05:59.359 --> 00:06:04.897
My sense when Congressman Bacon floated
that was it was in part to put pressure

00:06:04.931 --> 00:06:10.236
on this recalcitrant group
to understand that there was a time limit.

00:06:10.236 --> 00:06:14.774
And if they if they pushed down this road,
that they might end up with a situation

00:06:14.974 --> 00:06:15.775
like that.

00:06:15.775 --> 00:06:18.111
But executing it,
it just seems difficult to me.

00:06:18.111 --> 00:06:22.982
You're saying five Republicans would join
the Democrats to elect a speaker.

00:06:22.982 --> 00:06:23.616
Is that you're

00:06:24.751 --> 00:06:27.320
I think there's any number of ways
this could go down.

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:30.123
I think let's say you take Fred Upton,
name has been floated.

00:06:30.123 --> 00:06:31.758
He's a great guy. I'll just use him.

00:06:31.758 --> 00:06:33.693
I don't know
if he's actually interested in doing it.

00:06:33.693 --> 00:06:36.329
But Fred Upton, let's say
his name is put up.

00:06:36.362 --> 00:06:38.431
You can get every Democratic vote for him.

00:06:38.831 --> 00:06:40.800
And his agreement with the Democrats
is I'm just going

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:44.070
to create a level playing field
and then you get five Republicans.

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:46.072
It's possible
that that's how it could get done.

00:06:46.072 --> 00:06:47.774
Again, I think we have to go through

00:06:47.774 --> 00:06:51.677
a number of these iterations until people
realize it's doing real damage.

00:06:52.078 --> 00:06:53.212
But I wouldn't rule it out.

00:06:53.212 --> 00:06:55.281
And I think
it's a small percentage chance.

00:06:55.281 --> 00:06:56.916
But I do think, you know,

00:06:56.916 --> 00:06:59.218
when we were talking
about this possibility a month ago,

00:06:59.285 --> 00:07:01.421
it's certainly more possible
now than it was a month ago.

00:07:01.487 --> 00:07:05.558
Scott, Adam and Scott Jennings,
I have a question about your

00:07:05.558 --> 00:07:09.228
your views on the tolerance
of these members of Congress.

00:07:09.228 --> 00:07:11.063
You know,
they've gone through a couple of days now.

00:07:11.063 --> 00:07:13.232
They've adjourned until tomorrow.

00:07:13.533 --> 00:07:16.269
You know, who's to say anything
productive is going to happen tomorrow?

00:07:16.269 --> 00:07:19.005
Then you go through the weekend
and then we're into next week.

00:07:19.005 --> 00:07:22.475
At what point do you think fatigue begins
to alter the judgment

00:07:22.475 --> 00:07:23.276
of some of these folks?

00:07:23.276 --> 00:07:24.744
I mean, it's easy to be, you know,

00:07:24.744 --> 00:07:28.815
intransigent today or it's easy to say
I'm I'm all in on some outcome today.

00:07:29.148 --> 00:07:32.318
But by Tuesday or Wednesday of next week,
I was curious about your views of

00:07:32.318 --> 00:07:34.253
of their of their stamina for this.

00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:37.924
Well,
I fit for the average member of Congress.

00:07:37.957 --> 00:07:40.226
They're already there.
They're ready for this to be done.

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:42.895
You know,
you had family here. It's the weekend.

00:07:42.929 --> 00:07:44.597
There's a lot of pressure there.

00:07:44.597 --> 00:07:46.365
You know,
none of them will tell you on the air.

00:07:46.365 --> 00:07:48.267
They're not going to come out and say,
we're just tired.

00:07:48.267 --> 00:07:50.503
We need to go home,
because obviously you have to put up this.

00:07:50.503 --> 00:07:51.971
We're here to fight front.

00:07:51.971 --> 00:07:55.575
The question is,
then who's going to give first and if you

00:07:55.575 --> 00:07:57.877
look at the five or six.

00:07:57.877 --> 00:07:59.979
From what I know of them,
I've worked with them.

00:08:00.847 --> 00:08:02.648
They enjoy being in this moment.

00:08:02.648 --> 00:08:04.951
They enjoy being in this position.

00:08:04.951 --> 00:08:08.654
And so fatigue is certainly heading now
and it's going to get stronger.

